I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen PXRD workflow/tools, then build a chemically plausible phase inventory before refining candidates in GSAS-II. I’ll keep the refinement residual-led and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and `final.gpx` all agree.

The chemistry is a 1:1 CoO/WO₃ firing at 900 °C, so CoWO₄ is the leading product, but I’m treating residual CoO/WO₃, cobalt–tungsten intermediates, and polymorph/record alternatives as live candidates until the peak families and scales reject them. I’m now extracting the peak inventory and locating usable reference structures in the frozen COD.

The peak families already separate into a dominant room-temperature WO₃ pattern plus a wolframite-type CoWO₄ family; I’m testing CoO as a stoichiometrically plausible residual rather than assuming it. For CoWO₄, the CoMoO₄-II parent is the best physical analogue (same Co octahedra and P2/c framework, with Mo→W), and I’m comparing it against the ZnWO₄-derived alternate under the same restrained profile.

The initial two-phase model reaches Rwp ≈ 6.5%, while CoO refines to effectively zero scale; I’ve therefore rejected that specific CoO record for the provisional inventory. The first unconstrained width fit drove Gaussian U negative, so I’m rebuilding the comparison with U fixed at its physical boundary (0) and common nonnegative W/X before judging analogue or polymorph choices.

The physical-profile trial set is still refining. I’m comparing the CoMoO₄- and ZnWO₄-derived CoWO₄ models and monoclinic versus triclinic WO₃ with identical background, scales, shift, cells, and common width terms; this isolates structure/source choice from nuisance flexibility.

The triclinic WO₃ record currently fits better than the monoclinic alternate (Rwp 6.18% vs 6.56%) under the same physical profile, and both CoWO₄ parents converge to essentially the same cell. I’m finishing the remaining WO₃ polymorph and cobalt-oxide challenges before locking the inventory; any cobalt oxide must retain a positive scale and explain coherent unique line families to stay.

The alternate-source runs are computationally slower because each WO₃ cell has many independent metric terms. No candidate has been accepted on Rwp alone; I’ll also inspect whether residual peaks coincide across multiple non-overlapped families and whether any width parameter is compensating for the wrong symmetry.

The residuals show no coherent unindexed family: the remaining errors sit on modeled CoWO₄/WO₃ reflections and mainly change shape with angle. I’m now testing isotropic strain phase-by-phase against the common profile; this is the first justified nuisance expansion, since high-angle lines are visibly broader while several mid-angle peaks are over-broadened by the common-only model.

The strain comparison is decisive: WO₃ takes a positive isotropic microstrain (~2200 ppm) and improves Rwp to 6.00%, while CoWO₄ tries to refine a negative strain and is rejected. I’m checking crystallite-size terms next; any gain that depends on a negative/bound value or duplicates common X will be pruned.

Size and strain for WO₃ give nearly the same gain and are strongly correlated when combined, so I’m retaining only the simpler isotropic-size term for now. Polarization and roughness tried to leave their physical ranges and were rejected. I’m refining coordinates next, prioritizing W/Co sites because they dominate Cu‑Kα scattering and structural identifiability; oxygen-only detail will be accepted only if stable and physically screened.

Heavy-site coordinate shifts are chemically plausible (~0.02–0.04 Å scale) and improve the fit; unconstrained oxygen refinements move much farther and are being rejected as powder-data overfit despite lower Rwp. I’m combining only Co/W heavy-site refinements from both phases and checking that independent starting orders converge to the same model.

The independent heavy-site runs agree on fractions (~31.1% CoWO₄ / 68.9% WO₃) and profile parameters within small differences, though atomic shifts remain mildly correlated. I’m rechecking global intensity/asymmetry corrections after those structural changes; earlier polarization/roughness failures may have been compensating for unrefined heavy-atom coordinates.

Global polarization, roughness, and SH/L remain unsupported or nonphysical after the coordinate update, so they are pruned. I’m now doing the required texture challenge on CoWO₄ first across symmetry-distinct [100], [010], [001], [110], and [111] axes; a texture term will survive only if its ratio stays physical and improves several separated reflection families.

CoWO₄ texture tests produce only marginal, axis-ambiguous gains with March ratios close to 1, so none is retained. The analogous WO₃ axis set is running now; because triclinic symmetry makes these genuinely distinct, I’m looking for one clearly superior, replay-stable axis rather than accepting a small numerical improvement.

Neither phase shows defensible March–Dollase texture. The remaining family-dependent width mismatch is being challenged with uniaxial microstrain along [100], [010], and [001] for each phase, which matches the crystal symmetries and the observed angle-dependent broadening without adding arbitrary generalized terms.

The anisotropy trials are still converging because the triclinic phase has many split reflections. I’m requiring both axial and equatorial strain components to remain nonnegative; any apparent gain achieved by peak sharpening through a negative component will be discarded.

The Uiso test confirms the intensity trend is not a defensible thermal-displacement signal: both phases drive a common Uiso negative, so those gains are rejected. I’m resetting all atoms to a uniform small positive Uiso and checking sensitivity; this also removes inconsistent source-record ADPs (zero in WO₃ versus ~0.008–0.010 Å² in the analogue) without adding free parameters.

A fixed polarization of 1.0 is physically admissible and materially improves the fit (Rwp 5.24%); I’m retaining it but will flag that the unconstrained optimum lies above the physical limit. With that global trend removed, I’m re-testing oxygen coordinates once—if they still require large displacements, they will remain at the analogue/reference values.

The unrestrained oxygen trial still produces implausibly short W–O bonds (down to ~1.43 Å in WO₃), so it is rejected. I’m giving only CoWO₄ a restrained oxygen-coordinate test using its parent’s six octahedral bond lengths; this checks whether modest, chemically allowed oxygen shifts add real information without sacrificing geometry.

After rebuilding the inventory/profile, U now refines to a positive value (~32) with W and X also positive, so the common profile is physically adequate and slightly better (Rwp 5.22%). I’m re-challenging size and strain from this corrected baseline; earlier width conclusions are not being carried forward blindly.

From the rebuilt physical profile, WO₃ retains a stable isotropic size near 0.20 µm; CoWO₄ size is effectively infinite, CoWO₄ strain goes negative, and isotropic WO₃ strain destabilizes U. I’m doing the final anisotropy re-challenge now, with nonnegative U and strain components required simultaneously.

All anisotropic-strain variants fail the joint physical check (negative CoWO₄ components or negative common U for WO₃). The final texture screen is now running after polarization correction, which is the proper order for judging family-specific intensity effects.

The final texture screen is still converging across ten axis/phase combinations. I’ll retain a term only if one axis separates clearly from the others and the March ratio is meaningfully different from 1; otherwise the random-powder model remains the defensible choice.

No texture or anisotropic strain survives the corrected-profile screen. The accepted model is now being replayed through repeated full-range refinements with heavy-atom coordinates frozen, so only the supported background, scales, shift, cells, common U/W/X, and WO₃ isotropic size remain active.

The accepted model is internally stable (Δχ² below 0.001% on the last pass, no frozen/bound variables). I noticed the trial limits omitted the small edge intervals below 10° and above 100°, so I’m reconverging on the exact 9.9034–100.0949° observed grid before export; the final project and `fit.csv` will use every measured bin.

The full-range refinement has converged to a two-phase model: wolframite CoWO₄ plus triclinic WO₃, at roughly 30/70 wt%. I’m now doing the exact project/CIF/profile exports and replay checks so every submitted file agrees numerically with `final.gpx`.

The extra convergence pass exposed a shallow correlated profile minimum: it reduced the largest normalized shift but slightly worsened Rwp. I’m resolving that by comparing fresh atomic forward replays, rather than choosing from cached fit arrays or convergence flags alone.

The fresh replay now exactly reproduces the submitted project: Rwp = 5.19655%, both fractions match to machine precision, the whole 8,301-point grid is covered, and the verifier reports no negative-width/background/polarization flags. Both exported CIFs also match the structures regenerated from `final.gpx`. I’m finishing the concise evidence and uncertainty record.

Analysis complete. The modeled crystalline composition is:

- CoWO₄, wolframite P2/c: 29.77 wt%
- Triclinic WO₃, P-1: 70.23 wt%

Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. Final fit: Rwp 5.19655%, Rp 4.00211%, GOF 1.58669. Fresh GSAS-II replay reproduced the profile and fractions exactly with no physical-profile flags. Realistic fraction uncertainty is approximately ±2 wt%; oxygen coordinates are less certain than phase identities and heavy-atom frameworks.

